{"accessed_at":"2026-11-08T06:01:26.214Z","result":"base pdf dicto.indd (https://cdn.media.interlabdist.com.br/uploads/2021/01/Reinforced-Clostridial-Medium.pdf)\nciteturn89view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 2; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://cdn.media.interlabdist.com.br/uploads/2021/01/Reinforced-Clostridial-Medium.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 146\nL0@P0: Difco™ & BBL™ Manual, 2nd Edition\nL1@P0: Reinforced Clostridial Medium\nL2@P0: Intended Use\nL3@P0: Reinforced Clostridial Medium is used for cultivating and\nL4@P0: enumerating clostridia, other anaerobes, and other species of\nL5@P0: bacteria from foods and clinical specimens.\nL6@P0: Meets United States Pharmacopeia (USP), European Pharma\u0002copoeia (EP) and Japanese Pharmacopoeia (JP)1-3 performance\nL7@P0: specifications, where applicable.\nL8@P0: Summary and Explanation\nL9@P0: Reinforced Clostridial Medium is a semisolid medium formu\u0002lated by Hirsch and Grinstead.4\nL10@P0:  Their work demonstrated that\nL11@P0: the medium outperformed other media in supporting growth\nL12@P0: of clostridia from small inocula and produced higher viable cell\nL13@P0: counts.4 Barnes and Ingram5 used the medium to dilute vegeta\u0002tive cells of Clostridium perfringens. Barnes et al.6\nL14@P0:  used a solid\nL15@P0: (agar) version of the medium to enumerate clostridia in food.\nL16@P0: Reinforced Clostridial Medium is a nonselective enrichment\nL17@P0: medium and grows various anaerobic and facultative bacteria\nL18@P0: when incubated anaerobically.7\nL19@P0:  This medium has been used to\nL20@P0: detect clostridia, bifidobacteria and other anaerobes in food\nL21@P0: products8-11 and fecal samples.12 Reinforced Clostridial Medium\nL22@P0: is listed in the USP as the recommended medium for the isolation\nL23@P0: of Clostridium sp. from nonsterile pharmaceutical products.1\nL24@P0: Principles of the Procedure\nL25@P0: Reinforced Clostridial Medium contains peptone and beef\nL26@P0: extract as sources of carbon, nitrogen, vitamins and minerals.\nL27@P0: Yeast extract supplies B-complex vitamins which stimulate\nL28@P0: bacterial growth. Dextrose is the carbohydrate source. Sodium\nL29@P0: chloride maintains the osmotic balance. In low concentrations,\nL30@P0: soluble starch detoxifies metabolic by-products. Cysteine HCl\nL31@P0: is the reducing agent. Sodium acetate acts as a buffer. The small\nL32@P0: amount of agar makes the medium semisolid.\nL33@P0: User Quality Control\nL34@P0: Identity Specifications\nL35@P0: Difco™ Reinforced Clostridial Medium\nL36@P0: Dehydrated Appearance: Light tan, free-flowing, homogeneous.\nL37@P0: Solution: 3.8% solution, soluble in purified water upon\nL38@P0: boiling. Solution is medium amber, slightly\nL39@P0: opalescent with dark particles and flocculation\nL40@P0: when hot.\nL41@P0: Prepared Appearance: Upon cooling, medium amber and becomes\nL42@P0: more opalescent.\nL43@P0: Reaction of 3.8%\nL44@P0: Solution at 25°C: pH 6.8 ± 0.2\nL45@P0: BBL™ Reinforced Clostridial Medium (prepared)\nL46@P0: Appearance: Light to medium amber and opalescent with\nL47@P0: particles.\nL48@P0: Reaction at 25°C: pH 6.8 ± 0.2\nL49@P0: Cultural Response\nL50@P0: Difco™ Reinforced Clostridial Medium\nL51@P0: Prepare the medium per label directions. Inoculate and incubate tubes\nL52@P0: with caps tightened at 35 ± 2°C for 18-48 hours. Inoculate 100 mL bottles\nL53@P0: with C. sporogenes cultures and incubate with caps tightened at 30-35°C\nL54@P0: for 48 hours.\nL55@P0: ORGANISM ATCC™ INOCULUM CFU RECOVERY\nL56@P0: Bacteroides fragilis 23745 30-300 Good\nL57@P0: Clostridium botulinum 3502 30-300 Good\nL58@P0: Clostridium perfringens 13124 30-300 Good\nL59@P0: Clostridium sporogenes 19404 <100 Growth\nL60@P0: Clostridium sporogenes 11437 <100 Growth\nL61@P0: BBL™ Reinforced Clostridial Medium (prepared)\nL62@P0: Inoculate and incubate bottles with caps tightened at 30-35°C for up to\nL63@P0: 48 hours.\nL64@P0: ORGANISM ATCC™ INOCULUM CFU RECOVERY\nL65@P0: Clostridium perfringens 13124 10-100 Fair to good\nL66@P0: Clostridium sporogenes 19404 <100 Growth\nL67@P0: Clostridium sporogenes 11437 <100 Growth\nL68@P0: Formula\nL69@P0: Difco™ Reinforced Clostridial Medium\nL70@P0: Approximate Formula* Per Liter\nL71@P0: Peptone .................................................................... 10.0 g\nL72@P0: Beef Extract............................................................... 10.0 g\nL73@P0: Yeast Extract ............................................................... 3.0 g\nL74@P0: Dextrose ..................................................................... 5.0 g\nL75@P0: Sodium Chloride ......................................................... 5.0 g\nL76@P0: Soluble Starch............................................................. 1.0 g\nL77@P0: Cysteine HCl ............................................................... 0.5 g\nL78@P0: Sodium Acetate .......................................................... 3.0 g\nL79@P0: Agar ........................................................................... 0.5 g\nL80@P0: *Adjusted and/or supplemented as required to meet performance criteria.\nL81@P0: Precautions13\nL82@P0: 1. Biosafety Level 2 practices, containment equipment and facili\u0002ties are recommended for activities with clinical specimens of\nL83@P0: human or animal origin containing or potentially containing\nL84@P0-1: C. botulinum or C. tetani or their toxins.Difco™ & BBL™ Manual, 2nd Edition\nL85@P1: 2. Biosafety Level 3 practices, containment equipment and\nL86@P1: facilities are recommended for all manipulations of cultures\nL87@P1: of these organisms and for activities with a high potential for\nL88@P1: aerosol or droplet production, and those involving produc\u0002tion quantities of toxin.\nL89@P1: Directions for Preparation from\nL90@P1: Dehydrated Product\nL91@P1: 1. Suspend 38 g of the powder in 1 L of purified water. Mix\nL92@P1: thoroughly.\nL93@P1: 2. Heat with frequent agitation and boil for 1 minute to com\u0002pletely dissolve the powder.\nL94@P1: 3. Autoclave at 121°C for 15 minutes.\nL95@P1: 4. Test samples of the finished product for performance using\nL96@P1: stable, typical control cultures.\nL97@P1: Procedure\nL98@P1: For pharmaceutical samples, refer to the USP for details on sam\u0002ple collection and preparation for testing of nonsterile products.1\nL99@P1: Refer to USP General Chapter <62> for details on the examina\u0002tion of nonsterile products and the isolation of clostridia using\nL100@P1: Reinforced Clostridial Medium.1\nL101@P1: Expected Results\nL102@P1: After appropriate incubation time and temperature, subculture\nL103@P1: each tube or bottle to two Columbia Agar plates. Incubate under\nL104@P1: both aerobic and anaerobic conditions for 48 hours at 30-35°C\nL105@P1: to confirm the presence of anaerobic growth. After incubation of\nL106@P1: these plates, if isolates grow anaerobically only (with or without\nL107@P1: endospores) and are catalase negative, this indicates the presence\nL108@P1: of Clostridium sp.1 Perform other confirmatory biochemical\nL109@P1: testing as necessary.\nL110@P1: References\nL111@P1: 1. United States Pharmacopeial Convention, Inc. 2008. The United States pharmacopeia 31/The national\nL112@P1: formulary 26, Supp. 1, 8-1-08, online. United States Pharmacopeial Convention, Inc., Rockville, Md.\nL113@P1: 2. European Directorate for the Quality of Medicines and Healthcare. 2008. The European pharma\u0002copoeia, 6th ed., Supp. 1, 4-1-2008, online. European Directorate for the Quality of Medicines and\nL114@P1: Healthcare, Council of Europe, 226 Avenue de Colmar BP907-, F-67029 Strasbourg Cedex 1, France.\nL115@P1: 3. Japanese Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare. 2006. The Japanese pharmacopoeia, 15th ed.,\nL116@P1: online. Japanese Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare.\nL117@P1: 4. Hirsch and Grinstead. 1954. J. Dairy Res. 21:101.\nL118@P1: 5. Barnes and Ingram. 1956. J. Appl. Bacteriol. 19:117.\nL119@P1: 6. Barnes, Despaul and Ingram. 1963. J. Appl. Bacteriol. 26:415.\nL120@P1: 7. MacFaddin. 1985. Media for isolation-cultivation-identification-maintenance of medical bacteria,\nL121@P1: vol. 1. Williams & Wilkins, Baltimore, Md.\nL122@P1: 8. Mead. 1995. Principles involved in the detection and enumeration of clostridia in foods. In Corry, J.E.L.,\nL123@P1: et al. (eds.), Culture media for food microbiology. Elsevier Science B.V. Amsterdam, The Netherlands.\nL124@P1: 9. Roy. 2003. Media for the detection and enumeration of bifidobacteria in food products. In Corry, J.E.L.\nL125@P1: et al. (eds.), Handbook of culture media for food microbiology. Elsevier Science B.V. Amsterdam,\nL126@P1: The Netherlands.\nL127@P1: 10. Cocolin, Innocente, Biasutti and Giuseppe. 2004. Int. J. Food Microbiol. 90:83.\nL128@P1: 11. Health Canada. The compendium of analytical methods, online. Food Directorate, Health Products\nL129@P1: and food Branch, Health Canada, Ottawa, Ontario Canada.\nL130@P1: 12. Hartemink and Rombouts. 1999. J. Microbiol. Methods. 36:181.\nL131@P1: 13. U.S. Department of Health and Human Services. 2007. Biosafety in microbiological and biomedical\nL132@P1: laboratories, HHS Publication (CDC), 5th ed. U.S. Government Printing Office, Washington, D.C.\nL133@P1: Availability\nL134@P1: Difco™ Reinforced Clostridial Medium\nL135@P1: CCAM EP JP USP\nL136@P1: Cat. No. 218081 Dehydrated – 500 g†\nL137@P1: BBL™ Reinforced Clostridial Medium\nL138@P1: CCAM EP JP USP\nL139@P1: Cat. No. 215192 Prepared Bottles, 100 mL (septum screw cap) –\nL140@P1: Pkg. of 10†\nL141@P1: Europe\nL142@P1: CCAM EP\nL143@P1: Cat. No. 254548 Prepared Plates – Pkg. of 20*\nL144@P1: * Store at 2-8°C.\nL145@P1: † QC testing performed according to USP/EP/JP performance specifications.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn89view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.dilaco.com/dinamicos/productos/presentacion/PR_30-046330.pdf\",\"lineno\":130}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.dilaco.com/dinamicos/productos/presentacion/PR_30-046330.pdf: (400) Timeout fetching"}
